            <ArticleTitle>Kynurenic acid blocks chemogenic nociception.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>373-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Previous studies have suggested a role of excitatory amino acids (EAA) in nociception. In the present study the effects of two antagonists of EAA-receptors on capsaicin-induced eye wipings were investigated. Intracisternally administered kynurenic acid, in contrast to intravenously administered MK-801, was found to effectively block the induced eye wipings. It is proposed that EAA, by activating non-NMDA receptors, are involved in the transmission of chemogenic nociception.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
